<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # This Is Me! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is Me!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Trace simple shapes and letters. Interactive: Draw a symbol or image as a response to something.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can trace basic shapes and letters (e.g., "M" for me).
Can draw simple pictures of family members collaboratively (e.g., drawing a stick figure to represent "mom").
Can scribble with purpose to represent family-related ideas (e.g., writing simple lines and labeling the scribbles with the word "mom").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher uses large flashcards of "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister," and "baby." The teacher holds up a flashcard and asks, "Who is this?" Students respond by pointing and orally naming the family member (e.g., "Mom!"). The teacher praises correct answers and models the pronunciation of /m/ in "mom" and /s/ in "sister."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a crayon or marker correctly and demonstrates tracing a large letter 'M' on a whiteboard or large paper, saying "M for mom." The teacher emphasizes smooth, controlled lines. Then, the teacher models drawing a simple stick figure and says, "This is my mom."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a mom) "Is this dad or mom?" (Teacher encourages students to point or use a simple gesture for 'mom').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a crayon) "Do we draw with our hands or our feet?" (Teacher guides students to point to their hands).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Family")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful "My Family" poster showing simple illustrations of "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister," "baby," and a "dog" or "cat" with their names clearly written below them. A simple, illustrated storybook about "My Happy Family."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Naming family members and identifying them visually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to each family member on the poster and asks, "Who is this?" Students chorally respond with the correct name (e.g., "Dad!"). The teacher emphasizes the initial sound /d/ in "dad."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads a short picture story about a happy family. After showing a picture of a "brother," the teacher asks, "Is this a happy brother or a sad brother?" Students point to the corresponding emotion or nod 'happy.'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks students to "Point to my family" on the poster, indicating the entire group of figures.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Tracing simple shapes and letters, drawing simple representations of family members. Grammar: Possessive adjectives ("my"). Vocabulary: mom, dad, brother, sister, baby.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Tracing Letters):</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with large, dotted letters, specifically "M" for mom, "D" for dad, "B" for brother, and "S" for sister. Each letter is placed next to a corresponding picture of the family member. Students use thick crayons to trace over the dotted lines of each letter. The teacher walks around, providing individual assistance and modeling tracing for students who need help, saying, "Trace the 'M' for 'mom'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-Controlled Practice - Drawing):</b> Students receive another worksheet with simple outlines of stick figures or basic shapes (e.g., a large circle for a head, two lines for a body). Next to each outline, there is a picture of a family member. The task is to draw inside the outline to complete the simple picture of "my mom," "my dad," etc. The teacher encourages simple additions like eyes or a smile, saying, "Draw your happy mom!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Collaborative Drawing - Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student shows their drawing from Activity 2 to their partner. They point to their drawing and practice saying, "This is my mom," "This is my dad," or "This is my brother." The teacher monitors, prompting with "Who is this?" if students are quiet, encouraging the use of "my" and family vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Family")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "My Family Drawing Book." Each student receives a small booklet of blank pages (or pages with simple pre-printed outlines/dotted letters). Each page is dedicated to a family member (e.g., one page for mom, one for dad, etc.).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher guides students through each page. For the "mom" page, the teacher says, "Today we will draw our mom. I will draw a big circle for her face." The teacher models drawing a simple face and asks students to do the same.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below their drawing, students are instructed to either trace the dotted word "MOM" (provided by the teacher) or to scribble lines with purpose to represent the name. The teacher reminds them, "This is 'M' for 'mom.' Let's trace it!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This process is repeated for "dad," "brother," "sister," and "baby," and optionally "dog" or "cat," integrating vocabulary and the initial sound identification (e.g., /p/ in 'play' if they draw playing family members).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to say, "This is my mom!" or "I love my family!" as they draw. The teacher circulates, offering support and engaging students in conversation using WH-questions ("Who is this?") and encouraging them to name their family members.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students individually present one page from their "My Family Drawing Book" to the teacher or a small group. They show their drawing and attempt to trace the letter or scribble representing the name. They are encouraged to orally identify the family member (e.g., "My dad!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can trace basic shapes and letters with some control (e.g., "M," "D").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can draw a simple picture or symbol to represent a family member (e.g., a stick figure for "mom").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to scribble purposefully or trace a pre-written word.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies the family member orally (e.g., "mom," "dad") with assistance.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you draw your mom?" (Students show thumbs up/down or nod).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me your happy family drawing!" (Students proudly display their work).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you draw in your book today?" (Encourage students to point to their drawings).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who is in your family?" (Students point to their drawings of family members).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging students in the tracing and drawing activities? The multi-sensory approach with large crayons and visual prompts was effective for Pre-K learners.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to further develop early writing skills? Some students needed significant hand-over-hand guidance for tracing; more pre-writing warm-ups might be beneficial. More opportunities for scribbling with purpose, perhaps with color coding for different family members, could be added.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on individual writing (tracing/drawing). The next lesson (Mediation & Integration) will build on these personal family drawings, allowing students to present their booklets and verbally share about their "happy family" to a wider audience, integrating speaking and previous learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>